# TODO - Native x86-64 and AArch64 backends

## Objective

Implement native-code emitters for x86-64 and AArch64 behind one execution-IR
backend contract. Architecture-specific register allocation, instruction
selection, calling conventions, executable-memory handling, instruction-cache
synchronization, and hardening remain adapters rather than VM semantics.

## Acceptance

- x86-64 and AArch64 consume the same portable execution IR and independently
  pass cross-backend differential suites including executable-memory and
  instruction-cache edge cases.
- Observable state, I/O, termination, and diagnostics match the declared
  semantic profile across positive, boundary, and adversarial fixtures.
- Performance conclusions use equivalent workloads and report raw-sample
  provenance, resource budgets, dispersion/uncertainty, and failure/success
  behavior rather than only a best-case number.
- The declared scope contains no unresolved placeholder implementation or
  undocumented workaround required for this objective to function.
- Evidence is durable enough to move this TODO to `docs/todo/completed/` and
  remove the exact TODO heading without losing unfinished intent.

## Evidence

- Expected durable artifact surface: `vm/`, `execution/`, `tests/vm/`,
  `benchmarks/interpreter/`.
- Required evidence: semantic fixtures, state/I/O traces where diagnostic, and
  differential results against independent specification-conformant
  implementations; the historical interpreter is compared only on its documented
  agreement domain.
- Prerequisite completion evidence: `tiered-native-execution-engine`.
- Shared prerequisite evidence includes portable IR v1 and cache identity that
  distinguishes OS, ISA, backend/native ABI revisions, required features, and
  exact canonical IR even under forced bucket-digest collisions.
- A Clang C23 bootstrap backend now emits real untrusted x86-64/AArch64 Windows
  COFF object candidates from the same portable IR. This proves the shared
  host-code boundary and target split. Safe-Rust COFF admission now verifies
  machine/entry/section identity and rejects unresolved external dependencies;
  a minimal direct emitter/verifier owns deopt-only x86-64/AArch64 stubs with
  byte-exact semantic admission, and `direct-initial-halt` now supplies the
  first
  state-applying fast path for an exact one-step zero-state halt subset.
  `direct-halt-registers` revision 5 extends that halt-only surface across
  arbitrary 32-bit registers and full 64-bit counter observations. Independent
  495/564-byte fixtures bind counters above `u32::MAX` on both ISAs; historical
  revision, counter, and opcode mismatch fail closed. `direct-halt-fetch`
  revision 2 adds a VM-decoded graphical `v` live-in with independent
  535/628-byte
  objects, x86-64 atomic execution evidence, and AArch64 instruction decoding.
  `direct-non-graphical` revision 2 applies the same fetched-terminal guards to
  a
  VM-classified non-graphical live-in with 538/631-byte objects.
  `direct-no-operation` revision 2 adds the first non-terminal code-memory write
  and pointer advance with independent 557/658-byte objects, x86-64 atomic
  execution, and AArch64 instruction decoding. `direct-jump-data` revision 1
  adds
  two distinct code/data live-in guards, an exact 125-word footprint, and the
  first instruction-specific semantic data read with independent 564/699-byte
  objects. `direct-jump-code` revision 1 adds three distinct live-in guards,
  exact post-jump encryption, a 13-word footprint, and independent 622/731-byte
  objects. `direct-rotate` revision 1 adds two guarded live-ins, two exact
  guest-memory writes, three register commits, a 9-word footprint, and
  independent
  578/732-byte objects. `direct-crazy` revision 1 uses the same reviewed
  two-read/two-write machine shape with independent VM-owned `p`/Crazy
  admission, a 9-word footprint, and independent 577/731-byte objects.
  `direct-output` revision 1 adds output-pointer and strict capacity guards, an
  exact byte append, and independent 642/724-byte objects. `direct-input`
  revision 1 adds exact byte and EOF forms with independent 659/744-byte and
  634/715-byte objects. All eight instruction families now have reviewed direct
  x86-64/AArch64 templates, and every memory-backed template guards the exact
  key-bound IR footprint.
  Native ABI revision 1 now has one format-neutral Rust `repr(C)` call frame.
  Its tested 80-byte size and field offsets match both reviewed encoder
  families; typed status/termination values and borrowed-buffer construction
  fail closed. This does not link, allocate, invoke, or synchronize code.
  Complete normative traces can now project to exact one-step IR and form one
  profile/observation-continuous `VerifiedDirectSequencePlan`; a retained
  rotate/output case selects reviewed artifacts on both ISAs, while hidden
  deopt and partial sequences fail closed. Cache-aware sequence planning
  preserves exact-hit `Arc` identity, stages verified misses, and inserts only
  after full success. This does not fuse or invoke objects.
  Remaining fused-region emission, executable chaining, executable-memory
  integration, and instruction-cache behavior remain pending for this TODO.
- Performance evidence pending: raw measurements plus a reproducible
